## 🪝 2. Hook Inventory — The Enforcement Surface

ACS fires a hook at each agent decision point. This is the complete interception surface available to a Guardian Agent.

| Hook | Fires when | Context available | What a verdict can do |
|------|-----------|-------------------|-----------------------|
| **Input** | Agent receives input (user or retrieved content) | Content, source, provenance | Deny ingestion; strip/rewrite spans; tag as untrusted |
| **Output** | Agent emits a response | Response text, target audience | Redact; block; append risk labelling |
| **Tool selection** | Agent chooses which tools to consider | Candidate tool set, task context | Narrow the tool set before reasoning even begins |
| **Tool call** | Agent invokes a tool | Tool ID, arguments, invoking identity | Deny; rewrite arguments; require step-up approval |
| **Tool response** | Tool returns data | Payload, size, sensitivity | Redact; cap volume; block reingestion into memory |
| **Plan → execute transition** | Planning completes, execution begins | Signed task manifest vs. derived plan | Deny on goal drift — the single highest-value gate |
| **Memory store** | Agent writes to memory / RAG | Content, provenance, trust score | Deny low-provenance writes; force TTL; quarantine |
| **Code execution** | Agent runs generated code | Code, runtime, sandbox profile | Deny; force sandbox profile; strip network egress |
| **Sub-agent invocation** | Agent spawns or delegates to a peer | Callee identity, delegated scope, depth | Cap delegation depth; narrow inherited scope; deny |

> **The design insight:** these hooks map almost one-to-one onto the trust boundaries from Day 53. TB-4 (untrusted content) → Input hook. TB-2 (planner→executor) → Plan-transition hook. TB-3 (tools/memory) → Tool-call and Memory-store hooks. TB-5 (peer agents) → Sub-agent hook. The threat model tells you *where* the boundary is; ACS gives you *code that runs there*.

