## 🎯 3. ASI01–ASI10 → ACS Enforcement Mapping

The core deliverable. For each agentic risk: the hook that catches it, the policy that decides, the verdict that stops it, and the telemetry that proves it happened.

| ASI risk | Primary hook(s) | Policy logic | Verdict | Required telemetry |
|----------|-----------------|--------------|---------|--------------------|
| **ASI01** Agent Goal Hijack | Input, **Plan→execute** | Provenance-tag every input span; deny plan transition when derived plan diverges from the signed task manifest beyond threshold; untrusted spans cannot originate tool calls | `deny` on drift; `modify` to strip untrusted imperatives | Manifest hash, plan diff, originating span provenance |
| **ASI02** Tool Misuse & Exploitation | Tool selection, **Tool call** | Per-tool scope allowlist; argument semantic validation against expected types/ranges; destructive verb detection triggers step-up | `deny` unscoped; `modify` args; step-up on destructive | Tool ID, arg hash, scope decision, chain depth |
| **ASI03** Identity & Privilege Abuse | **Sub-agent**, Tool call | Every agent has a bounded identity; sub-agents receive fresh narrower credentials — never parent token passthrough; delegation depth capped | `deny` on passthrough or depth breach | Agent identity, delegation chain, credential TTL |
| **ASI04** Agentic Supply Chain | Tool selection, **AgBOM** | Verify signature on every tool descriptor / MCP manifest at load; registry allowlist; no runtime discovery of unsigned tools | `deny` unsigned or unpinned | AgBOM delta event, signature verification result |
| **ASI05** Unexpected Code Execution | **Code execution** | Hard separation of generation and execution; forced sandbox profile (non-root, egress-deny); dependency pre-analysis | `deny`; `modify` to enforce sandbox profile | Code hash, sandbox profile, dependency set |
| **ASI06** Memory & Context Poisoning | **Memory store**, Tool response | Write-time validation; provenance score threshold; mandatory TTL; block auto-reingestion of agent output into trusted memory | `deny` below trust threshold; `modify` to add TTL | Write source, provenance score, TTL, segment ID |
| **ASI07** Insecure Inter-Agent Comms | **Sub-agent**, Tool call | mTLS required; message signature + nonce validated; schema and version enforced; authenticated discovery only | `deny` unauthenticated or replayed | Peer identity, signature status, nonce, route |
| **ASI08** Cascading Failures | Plan→execute, Tool call, Sub-agent | Blast-radius cap per workflow; circuit breaker on error rate; task-scoped one-time credentials; loop detection | `deny` past cap; breaker trips workflow | Action count, fan-out degree, breaker state, lineage |
| **ASI09** Human-Agent Trust Exploitation | **Output**, Tool call | Irreversible actions require multi-step human approval; output carries risk badge + confidence; rationale logged immutably | `modify` to attach risk labelling; step-up gate | Rationale text, approval chain, risk tier |
| **ASI10** Rogue Agents | All hooks (**behavioural**) | Signed behavioural manifest; continuous verification of observed vs. declared behaviour; watchdog scoring; kill switch | `deny` all on manifest violation; revoke credentials | Behavioural baseline delta, watchdog score, kill event |

**Read the pattern:** six of ten risks are stopped at exactly two hooks — **tool call** and **memory store**. If an organization can only instrument two hooks in the first quarter, those are the two. That is a genuinely actionable prioritization, and it falls out of the mapping rather than out of opinion.

