## 📡 5. Telemetry Specification

ACS extends OpenTelemetry with agent semantic conventions and maps security events to OCSF for SIEM ingestion — deliberately contributing upstream rather than maintaining a competing trace standard. Minimum viable span attributes per hook:

| Attribute | Example | Why a governance function needs it |
|-----------|---------|-----------------------------------|
| `acs.agent.id` | `agent://finops-recon/v3` | Attribution — the ASI03 repudiation gap |
| `acs.agent.identity` | non-human principal ID | Proves no shared service account |
| `acs.task.manifest_hash` | `sha256:9f2c…` | Anchor for goal-drift detection (ASI01) |
| `acs.plan.divergence` | `0.12` | Quantified intent deviation |
| `acs.hook.name` | `tool_call` | Which control point fired |
| `acs.verdict` | `deny` | The enforcement decision itself |
| `acs.policy.id` | `P-TOOL-02` | Traces the decision to a reviewable rule |
| `acs.tool.id` / `.args_hash` | `mcp://jira/issue.delete` | Action forensics without logging payloads |
| `acs.span.provenance` | `untrusted` | Injection-path reconstruction (ASI01/06) |
| `acs.delegation.chain` | `[a1, a2]` | Privilege-escalation path (ASI03) |
| `acs.blast.action_count` | `37` | Cascade containment evidence (ASI08) |
| `acs.rationale.ref` | span pointer | Human-oversight defensibility (ASI09) |

**The forensic requirement Day 53 identified was intent deviation telemetry.** `manifest_hash` + `plan.divergence` + `span.provenance` is exactly that, expressed in fields a SIEM can already alert on. This is the line where "we should monitor for goal drift" becomes a detection rule.

