## ⚠️ 7. Coverage Gap Analysis (Honest Version)

ACS is the strongest available answer to runtime agent control. It is not a complete governance program. What it does *not* solve:

| Gap | Why ACS doesn't close it | Where it must be closed |
|-----|--------------------------|-------------------------|
| **Plan-divergence scoring** | ACS gives the hook; the *semantics* of "has this plan drifted" are yours to define and tune | Enterprise Tier 3 classifier + red-team calibration |
| **Provenance at ingestion** | ACS can act on a provenance tag; it does not manufacture one. If your retrieval layer doesn't tag, there's nothing to enforce on | Data pipeline and RAG layer — upstream of the agent |
| **Adoption dependency** | Tier 1 requires the *platform* to expose hooks. Un-instrumented frameworks are unenforceable, full stop | Vendor requirement in procurement — make hooks a buying criterion |
| **Fail-open defaults** | A Guardian Agent that times out and allows the action is theatre | Explicit `fail_mode: deny` in every step-up policy |
| **Human oversight quality** | ACS can require an approval. It cannot prevent approval fatigue — which is precisely the ASI09 attack | Rate-limit approvals; batch review; UX design |
| **Organisational accountability** | No standard assigns ownership, risk tiering, or impact assessment | ISO 42001 Cl. 6.1.2; EU AI Act Art. 9; named owner |
| **v0.1 maturity** | Deny/modify support for MCP and A2A is roadmapped at v3, not shipped | Compensating controls at the gateway layer today |

**The load-bearing caveat:** ACS moves the control plane out of the model and into inspectable infrastructure. That is a genuine architectural improvement. It does not make the hard judgment calls — what counts as drift, what is irreversible, what risk tier applies — go away. Those remain governance decisions, and they are the ones a VP actually owns.

